          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Materials: What Is Touching Your Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the specification we would check first, and the one manufacturers describe least clearly. Many kettles have a stainless exterior and plastic components inside — a lid liner, a water gauge, a filter housing — all of which sit in contact with boiling water several times a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Full stainless interiors</strong> are the straightforward answer: durable, inert, and available on kettles as inexpensive as the Cosori. Check descriptions for phrases like "stainless interior" and look at photographs of the inside of the lid, because that is where plastic most often hides.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Borosilicate glass</strong> is equally inert and lets you see the water level and the state of the interior, which encourages descaling. The trade is fragility and that every mineral deposit is visible, so a glass kettle looks neglected sooner than a steel one that is equally scaled inside.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Plastic water paths</strong> are common at the bottom of the market. Modern kettles use food-safe polymers, but repeated boiling does degrade plastics over years, and many buyers simply prefer not to. Given that stainless alternatives exist for very little more money, there is not much reason to accept it.
          </p>

          <p>
            One aside on taste: a new kettle of any material often produces slightly odd-tasting water for the first few boils. Fill it, boil it, discard the water, and repeat two or three times before using it — this is normal and it resolves.
          </p>
