        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="limescale" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Limescale: The Thing That Kills Kettles
          </h2>

          <p>
            In a hard water area, calcium carbonate deposits on the heating element within weeks. The visible result is white flakes in your tea; the invisible result is an insulating layer between the element and the water, which makes the kettle slower, less efficient, and eventually causes it to fail. Descaling is the single maintenance task that determines how long a kettle lasts.
          </p>

          <p>
            The method is simple and cheap. Fill the kettle with equal parts white vinegar and water, or use a sachet of citric acid, bring it to the boil, and leave it to stand for twenty to thirty minutes. Pour it out, wipe any loosened deposit away, then boil two or three kettles of clean water and discard them to clear the taste. In hard water, do this monthly; in soft water, every three months is ample.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two habits reduce how often you need to. Empty the kettle after use rather than leaving water standing, since scale forms as water evaporates and concentrates minerals. And if your water is very hard, using filtered water in the kettle noticeably slows deposit formation — our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">water filter pitcher guide</a> covers the cheapest way to do that.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing to avoid: abrasive scouring of the interior or the element. Scratching the surface gives future scale something to key into, which makes the problem worse rather than better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety Features Worth Having
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Automatic shutoff</strong> is standard on virtually everything sold now, cutting power when the water boils. <strong>Boil-dry protection</strong> is the more important one and is not universal: it shuts the element off if the kettle is switched on empty, which is the single most common way a kettle is destroyed and a genuine fire risk in a cheaply built unit. Confirm it is present before buying anything inexpensive.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cool-touch exteriors</strong> matter in households with small children. A stainless single-wall kettle gets hot enough to burn, and a curious child reaching for a shiny object on a counter is a predictable scenario. Double-wall designs stay comfortable to touch, and a cordless kettle stored towards the back of the counter with the cord tucked away removes the pulling hazard.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A secure lid</strong> is worth checking in person if you can. A lid that pops open when the kettle is tipped past a certain angle can dump boiling water, and it is a surprisingly common flaw in poorly made kettles. Related: never open the lid immediately after boiling to look inside, because the steam release is what scalds people, not the water.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more practical point for households with young children or older relatives: weight when full. A 1.7 litre kettle holds nearly four pounds of boiling water, and pouring that accurately across a counter is not a small task for everyone. A smaller kettle, or a dispenser that pours at the press of a button without being lifted at all, is a genuine safety improvement rather than a convenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check the base. A cordless kettle should seat firmly on its power base with no rocking, and a kettle that can be knocked off its base with a brush of the arm is a hazard regardless of what other protections it has.
          </p>
        </div>


