            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best electric kettle in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Fellow Stagg EKG is our overall pick for precision — the best pour control here, accurate temperature to the degree, and a fully stainless water path. For a household that drinks different teas, the Breville IQ Kettle Pure's presets and 1.7 litre capacity make more sense. For most people, the Cosori Speed-Boil does the core job faster than kettles costing three times as much.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are variable temperature kettles worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you drink green or white tea or make pour-over coffee, yes. Boiling water scorches delicate leaves and produces bitterness people often blame on the tea, and coffee extracts best between 195 and 205 degrees F rather than at a full boil. If you drink black tea and instant coffee exclusively, a simple boil-only kettle is all you need.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do electric kettles use a lot of electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They use a lot of power briefly, which is different. A 1500-watt kettle running for two minutes uses about 0.05 kWh — roughly a cent of electricity. Because the element sits in the water, around 80 percent of the energy reaches it, making an electric kettle the most efficient way to boil water in a domestic kitchen. Boiling only what you need saves more than any model choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a plastic electric kettle safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Modern kettles use food-safe polymers and are sold as safe, but many people prefer not to have plastic in contact with boiling water several times a day, and plastics do degrade over years of repeated heating. Since stainless-interior kettles start at very modest prices, there is little reason to accept a plastic water path. Check inside the lid, which is where plastic most often hides.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I descale an electric kettle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fill it with equal parts white vinegar and water, or use a citric acid sachet, boil it, and leave it to stand for twenty to thirty minutes. Pour out, wipe away loosened deposit, then boil and discard two or three kettles of fresh water to clear the taste. Monthly in hard water areas, every three months in soft. Never scour the element — scratches make future scale worse.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size electric kettle should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                0.6 to 1.0 litres for one person or a coffee-focused kitchen, 1.5 to 1.7 litres for a couple or a family, and a 3 litre dispenser if your household drinks tea all day. Bigger is not better: boiling a full 1.7 litre kettle for one cup wastes time and electricity, and most people are better served by a smaller kettle they fill appropriately.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a gooseneck kettle for coffee?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For pour-over, it makes a real difference — the narrow spout lets you wet the grounds evenly rather than digging a crater, which is most of what separates consistent pour-over from inconsistent. For French press, drip machines, or tea, it offers nothing and actively slows down filling a teapot. Buy one only if pour-over is a regular part of your routine.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

