        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Electric Kettle Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A kettle is a simple appliance, which is exactly why the errors people make buying one tend to be about the details nobody advertises.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Checking What Touches the Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plenty of kettles have a stainless exterior and plastic inside the lid, around the water gauge, or in the filter housing. Since stainless-interior kettles are available cheaply, look at photographs of the inside before buying rather than assuming the outside material continues within.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Biggest Capacity Available</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boiling 1.7 litres for one mug of tea wastes several minutes and unnecessary electricity, every time. Buy for how you actually drink, and if you mostly make one or two cups, a smaller kettle is both faster and cheaper to run.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Descaling Until It Is Slow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scale on the element insulates it, which makes the kettle progressively slower and eventually kills it. A twenty-minute vinegar soak once a month in hard water keeps a kettle performing like new for years, and it is the difference between a three-year kettle and a ten-year one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Gooseneck as the Only Kettle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The narrow spout that gives precision for pour-over coffee pours slowly by design, which is frustrating when filling a teapot or a pot for pasta. If one kettle has to serve the whole household, a variable-temperature kettle with a normal spout is the better compromise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Boil-Dry Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Switching on an empty kettle is the most common way one is destroyed, and in a cheaply built unit it is a genuine fire risk. Automatic shutoff at boiling and boil-dry protection are different features — confirm the second is present, particularly on inexpensive models.
              </p>
